  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Construct a piecewise constant continuous-time signal by converting its interval-by-interval level changes into a sum of scaled shifted unit steps.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this section, the move is to encode a piecewise constant signal as one baseline value plus a sequence of level jumps that each turn on at specific times. Your target is to represent interval-by-interval constants with shifted unit-step terms so each breakpoint maps directly to one term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the unit step as the building cue: $u(t)$ is $0$ for $t<0$ and $1$ for $t\\ge 0$. A shifted version $u(t-a)$ moves that switch to time $a$, so it is $0$ for $t<a$ and $1$ for $t\\ge a$. Treat this as the fundamental left-right region rule you will reuse throughout.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case, use one transition from level $0$ to level $A$ at time $a$ with $y(t)=A u(t-a)$. Verify directly: when $t<a$, $u(t-a)=0$ so $y(t)=0$; when $t\\ge a$, $u(t-a)=1$ so $y(t)=A$. This one-term form is the smallest nontrivial pattern to imitate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To create a finite window, keep the first switch-on term and add a second switch-off term at the ending time $b$ as $y(t)=A u(t-a)-A u(t-b)$. In $a\\le t<b$, only the first term stays on and $y(t)=A$; for $t\\ge b$, both terms are on and cancel, giving $y(t)=0$. This is the same representation idea with one extra breakpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=y_0+\\sum_k \\Delta A_k u(t-a_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the next sections, keep the exact same action rule and only change the jump list: for each breakpoint at $a_k$, add $\\Delta A_k u(t-a_k)$ where $\\Delta A_k$ is the signed change from the prior level to the next level. Multiple transitions become a reusable sum, not separate special cases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Turning On a Constant Level with a Shifted Unit Step",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write a unit-step form for $y(t)=0$ for $t<2$, and $y(t)=5$ for $t\\ge 2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a two-region piecewise constant signal with zero baseline before a single switch time. In this form, the move is to represent the right-hand jump at $t=2$ as a shifted unit-step term added to the baseline.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-term template from the core move: $y(t)=A u(t-a)$ for a transition from $0$ to a level $A$ at time $a$. Here the start is at $a=2$ and the level after the switch is $A=5$, so the candidate form is $y(t)=5u(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=5u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Verify region-by-region to confirm the representation: for $t<2$, $u(t-2)=0$, so $y(t)=5\\cdot 0=0$. For $t\\ge 2$, $u(t-2)=1$, so $y(t)=5\\cdot 1=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is complete: the step form is $y(t)=5u(t-2)$, and it is the direct one-step extension of the previous tutorial pattern from section 1.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Turning Off a Constant Level with a Second Shifted Unit Step",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Construct a unit-step equation for $x(t)=4$ when $1\\le t<5$, and $x(t)=0$ elsewhere.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This signal has one nonzero pulse, so the cue is a finite interval with a clear start and end time. Use a turn-on term at $t=1$ and a turn-off term at $t=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the finite support pattern, place $4u(t-1)$ for the onset at the left edge. Then add a negative offset at the right edge: $-4u(t-5)$ to remove the level after $t\\ge 5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=4u(t-1)-4u(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check all regions. For $t<1$, both shifted steps are $0$, so $x(t)=0-0=0$. For $1\\le t<5$, $u(t-1)=1$ and $u(t-5)=0$, so $x(t)=4\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0=4$. For $t\\ge 5$, both are $1$, so $x(t)=4\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The finite support form is therefore $x(t)=4u(t-1)-4u(t-5)$, which is the same rule as before plus one extra breakpoint that turns the level off.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Changing Directly Between Two Nonzero Constant Levels",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert the piecewise signal $x(t)=2$ for $t<1$, $x(t)=5$ for $1\\le t<3$, and $x(t)=2$ for $t\\ge3$ to unit-step form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a nonzero-to-nonzero level change: the baseline is $2$, then the signal rises at $t=1$ and falls back at $t=3$. Because the baseline is already $2$, this is a cumulative-jump form problem, not a zero-reset problem.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set the baseline term first: $y_0=2$. Then compute each signed jump from the previous level. At $t=1$, the jump is $\\Delta A_1=5-2=3$, so add $3u(t-1)$. At $t=3$, the jump is $\\Delta A_2=2-5=-3$, so add $-3u(t-3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2+3u(t-1)-3u(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Validate each interval. For $t<1$, both $u(t-1)$ and $u(t-3)$ are $0$, so $x(t)=2+0-0=2$. For $1\\le t<3$, only $u(t-1)=1$ and $u(t-3)=0$, so $x(t)=2+3-0=5$. For $t\\ge3$, both are $1$, so $x(t)=2+3-3=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final step form is $x(t)=2+3u(t-1)-3u(t-3)$, which shows the exact signed-jump mechanism for direct transitions between two nonzero levels.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Constant Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Combining Multiple Level Changes into One Step Expression",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write one unit-step expression for $x(t)=1$ if $t<0$, $x(t)=4$ if $0\\le t<2$, $x(t)=-2$ if $2\\le t<4$, and $x(t)=3$ if $t\\ge4$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This signal has multiple breakpoints, so the cue is to use one baseline and one signed jump at each breakpoint. List the levels in order: start at $1$, then jump to $4$ at $t=0$, then to $-2$ at $t=2$, then to $3$ at $t=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set the baseline to the first value: $y_0=1$. The first jump is $\\Delta A_1=4-1=3$ at $t=0$, so add $3u(t)$. The second jump is $\\Delta A_2=-2-4=-6$ at $t=2$, so add $-6u(t-2)$. The third jump is $\\Delta A_3=3-(-2)=5$ at $t=4$, so add $5u(t-4)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1+3u(t)-6u(t-2)+5u(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the intervals. For $t<0$, all shifted steps are $0$, giving $x(t)=1$. For $0\\le t<2$, only $u(t)=1$, so $x(t)=1+3=4$. For $2\\le t<4$, $u(t)=1$ and $u(t-2)=1$ while $u(t-4)=0$, so $x(t)=1+3-6=-2$. For $t\\ge4$, all terms are $1$, so $x(t)=1+3-6+5=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The compact representation is $x(t)=1+3u(t)-6u(t-2)+5u(t-4)$, and it is the general reusable form for multiple level changes in this topic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
